Adaptive Reasoning Value Engine:

A Decision-Theoretic Foundation for Optimal Reasoning

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek,Grok XAi, Claude 

Abstract

We present a mathematical framework that treats reasoning as a costly internal action whose utility can be evaluated by the principles of optimal stopping. The agent's cognitive state is formalised as a state in a semi-Markov decision process augmented with reasoning operations. The Future Reasoning Value (FRV) of an operation is defined as the net gain in optimal future utility produced by executing that operation, minus its computational cost. We prove that the optimal policy reduces to a simple threshold rule: continue reasoning if and only if \max_r \mathrm{FRV}(r;R) > 0. The framework yields several rigorous results: (i) a uniqueness theorem for the value function via contraction mapping on a suitably regularised space, (ii) a quantitative impossibility theorem establishing a minimax lower bound on the regret of any static reasoning budget, thereby proving the necessity of adaptive controllers, (iii) a complete characterisation of diminishing reasoning returns through submodularity of the terminal utility with a fully explicit inductive proof of preservation under the Bellman operator, and (iv) a unified decision-theoretic calculus in which reasoning, memory, questioning, and belief update are all governed by the same principle of maximising future cognitive value. Learning FRV from process-reward models is discussed, together with a rigorous finite-sample bound on the bias introduced by fixed-horizon training and its correction via importance sampling. We provide an end-to-end sample-complexity guarantee from data to policy regret. The theory establishes a normative standard against which heuristic reasoning strategies can be measured and offers a rigorous foundation for the growing empirical literature on adaptive inference-time computation.

