2. The Reasoning-Augmented Decision Process

We now present the core mathematical framework. All value functions are bounded, the state space is Polish, and costs are strictly positive. Proofs are either given in full or sketched with the complete details deferred to the appendices of the full manuscript.

2.1 State Space, Operations, and Terminal Utility

An agent interacts with an external environment and, before committing to an external action, may perform a sequence of internal reasoning steps. The agent's internal state is denoted by R \in \mathcal{R}, where \mathcal{R} is a Polish space endowed with its Borel \sigma-algebra. The terminal utility of stopping in state R and acting optimally in the external world is described by a bounded measurable function

U : \mathcal{R} \to [0,1].

At each reasoning step the agent selects a reasoning operation r from a finite set \mathcal{O}. The operation updates the internal state, possibly stochastically. We write the transition as

R \xrightarrow{r} R \oplus r,

where R \oplus r denotes the random successor state drawn according to a Borel-measurable probability kernel P(\cdot \mid R, r). To simplify notation in the main text we often write deterministic updates; the extension to stochastic transitions replaces V(R \oplus r) with \mathbb{E}[V(R \oplus r) \mid R, r]. Every operation incurs a non-negative cost

c(R, r) \ge c_{\min} > 0,

which reflects time, computation, or energy expenditure. After the state update the agent may either stop and collect U(R \oplus r) or continue with another reasoning step.

Because reasoning steps are internal and do not alter the external discounting structure, we model the combined process as an undiscounted discrete-time semi-Markov decision process with positive per-step costs. The optimal value function V : \mathcal{R} \to \mathbb{R} satisfies the Bellman equation

\boxed{ V(R) = \max\Bigl\{ U(R),\; \max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \bigl[ -c(R, r) + \mathbb{E}[V(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] \bigr] \Bigr\}. } \tag{1}

The absence of a discount factor is deliberate: positive costs guarantee almost-sure finite termination, and the absence of discounting yields the cleanest form of the optimal stopping rule. For completeness, the discounted formulation with \gamma \in (0,1) is treated as a remark in the analysis of long-horizon external tasks; the two formulations coincide in the limit \gamma \uparrow 1.

2.2 Well-Posedness via Weighted Contraction

Without discounting, the Bellman operator in (1) is not a contraction in the supremum norm. To establish existence and uniqueness of the value function, we introduce a weighted norm that accounts for the positive cost structure.

Definition 2.1 (Weighted Supremum Norm). Fix a strictly positive weight function w : \mathcal{R} \to [1, \infty) satisfying the Lyapunov-type condition

\mathbb{E}[w(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] \le \lambda w(R), \qquad \lambda \in (0,1),

for all R \in \mathcal{R} and r \in \mathcal{O}. Such a weight exists whenever the reasoning steps incur a positive cost and the state space admits a suitable Lyapunov function (e.g., a function that grows with the number of reasoning steps taken). Define the weighted supremum norm

\|V\|_w = \sup_{R \in \mathcal{R}} \frac{|V(R)|}{w(R)}.

Let \mathcal{B}_w be the Banach space of measurable functions with finite \|\cdot\|_w-norm.

Theorem 2.2 (Well-Posedness). Assume U is bounded and measurable, c \ge c_{\min} > 0, and a Lyapunov weight w satisfying Definition 2.1 exists. Then the Bellman operator T defined by the right-hand side of (1) is a contraction of modulus \lambda on \mathcal{B}_w. Consequently, there exists a unique V \in \mathcal{B}_w satisfying V = TV, and an optimal stationary policy \pi^* exists and is Borel measurable.

Proof. Let V_1, V_2 \in \mathcal{B}_w. For any R \in \mathcal{R},

\begin{aligned}
|(TV_1)(R) - (TV_2)(R)|
&\le \max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \Bigl| \mathbb{E}[V_1(R \oplus r) - V_2(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] \Bigr| \\
&\le \max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathbb{E}\bigl[ |V_1(R \oplus r) - V_2(R \oplus r)| \mid R, r \bigr] \\
&\le \max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathbb{E}\bigl[ w(R \oplus r) \cdot \|V_1 - V_2\|_w \mid R, r \bigr] \\
&\le \lambda w(R) \|V_1 - V_2\|_w.
\end{aligned}

Dividing by w(R) and taking the supremum over R yields \|TV_1 - TV_2\|_w \le \lambda \|V_1 - V_2\|_w. Since \lambda < 1, T is a contraction. The Banach fixed-point theorem yields a unique fixed point V. Because the action set is finite and the objective is continuous in the value, a standard measurable selection argument (Kuratowski & Ryll-Nardzewski, 1965) provides a Borel measurable policy that attains the maximum; this policy is optimal by the verification theorem for undiscounted SMDPs with positive costs. \square

An alternative and perhaps more familiar route is to work with the discounted problem for \gamma \in (0,1), where the standard contraction argument in the supremum norm applies immediately, and then take the limit \gamma \uparrow 1. The positivity of costs ensures that the undiscounted value function is the pointwise limit of the discounted value functions, and optimal policies converge along subsequences. Both constructions are equivalent; we adopt the weighted-norm approach for its directness.

Lemma 2.3 (Almost-Sure Finite Termination). Every optimal policy \pi^* terminates after a finite number of reasoning steps almost surely.

Proof. If reasoning continued forever on a set of positive probability, the cumulative undiscounted cost would diverge to -\infty while U remains bounded in [0,1], yielding an expected return of -\infty. The policy that stops immediately attains a finite expected return \ge 0, contradicting optimality. \square

