3. Future Reasoning Value and Optimal Stopping

3.1 Definition and Decomposition

The central quantity of interest is the marginal net gain of executing an additional reasoning operation, accounting for the option to continue reasoning afterwards.

Definition 3.1 (Future Reasoning Value). For a state R \in \mathcal{R} and operation r \in \mathcal{O},

\boxed{ \mathrm{FRV}(r; R) := \mathbb{E}[V(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] - U(R) - c(R, r). } \tag{2}

FRV compares the expected value attainable by taking one more reasoning step and then behaving optimally with the value of stopping immediately, after subtracting the immediate cost. The continuation value after executing r is already incorporated in V(R \oplus r).

Connection to the classical Value of Computation. Russell and Wefald's Value of Computation for a computation \psi in belief state b is \mathrm{VOC}(\psi) = U(b \circ \psi) - U(b) - \text{cost}(\psi), measuring the immediate improvement in decision quality. FRV generalises this to sequential reasoning by adding the continuation value:

\mathrm{FRV}(r; R) = \underbrace{\bigl( \mathbb{E}[U(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] - U(R) - c(R, r) \bigr)}_{\text{VOC of } r} + \underbrace{\mathbb{E}\bigl[ V(R \oplus r) - U(R \oplus r) \mid R, r \bigr]}_{\text{continuation value}}. \tag{3}

The continuation value is non-negative and captures the option-like nature of sequential thinking: even if the immediate VOC is negative, the operation may still be worthwhile if it enables valuable future reasoning.

3.2 Optimal Stopping Rule

Theorem 3.2 (Optimal Stopping Rule). The optimal policy \pi^* satisfies

\pi^*(R) =
\begin{cases}
\text{stop} & \text{if } \displaystyle\max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{FRV}(r; R) \le 0, \\[1.2em]
\text{continue with } r^* = \arg\max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{FRV}(r; R) & \text{if } \displaystyle\max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{FRV}(r; R) > 0.
\end{cases} \tag{4}

Proof. From the Bellman equation (1), V(R) = U(R) exactly when

\max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \bigl( -c(R, r) + \mathbb{E}[V(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] \bigr) \le U(R).

Subtracting U(R) from both sides and using the definition of FRV yields the equivalent condition \max_r \mathrm{FRV}(r; R) \le 0. When the maximum is strictly positive, the Bellman equation selects the reasoning operation that attains it, which is precisely the maximiser of FRV. \square

The rule is conceptually simple: reason while there exists a reasoning step whose marginal benefit exceeds its cost, and stop otherwise. This is the unique solution to the optimal stopping problem.

