4. Diminishing Returns and Submodularity

In many reasoning tasks the marginal utility of additional steps declines as more knowledge is accumulated. We formalise this as a structural property of the terminal utility and prove that it is preserved by the optimal value function.

4.1 Information Order and Diminishing Returns

Let R \preceq S denote that S can be reached from R by a finite sequence of reasoning operations. Formally, \preceq is the transitive closure of the relation \{(R, R \oplus r) : R \in \mathcal{R}, r \in \mathcal{O}\}. This is a partial order on \mathcal{R} when the state transitions are acyclic (as is typical for reasoning processes that accumulate information).

Assumption 4.1 (Diminishing Returns of Terminal Utility). For all R \preceq S and every operation r \in \mathcal{O},

\mathbb{E}[U(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] - U(R) \;\ge\; \mathbb{E}[U(S \oplus r) \mid S, r] - U(S). \tag{5}

Condition (5) states that the expected marginal benefit of a reasoning step, measured with respect to the terminal value U, does not increase when more reasoning has already been performed. It is a discrete analogue of concavity and is closely related to submodularity when reasoning states are viewed as sets of accumulated inferences. This assumption holds exactly in Bayesian evidence-accumulation models with log-concave likelihoods and in many structured reasoning domains where later steps refine rather than revolutionise conclusions.

4.2 Preservation under the Bellman Operator

Lemma 4.2 (Preservation of Diminishing Returns). Assume that for each r \in \mathcal{O} the cost c(R, r) = c(r) depends only on the operation and that U satisfies Assumption 4.1. Then the optimal value function V also satisfies

\mathbb{E}[V(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] - V(R) \;\ge\; \mathbb{E}[V(S \oplus r) \mid S, r] - V(S) \qquad \text{for all } R \preceq S. \tag{6}

Proof. We prove by induction that each iterate of value iteration preserves the property. Define V_0 = U and

V_{k+1}(R) = \max\Bigl\{ U(R),\; \max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \bigl[ -c(r) + \mathbb{E}[V_k(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] \bigr] \Bigr\}.

The base case k = 0 holds by Assumption 4.1. Assume V_k satisfies (6) and fix R \preceq S and r \in \mathcal{O}. We must show

\mathbb{E}[V_{k+1}(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] - V_{k+1}(R) \;\ge\; \mathbb{E}[V_{k+1}(S \oplus r) \mid S, r] - V_{k+1}(S).

To simplify notation, let f_R = V_{k+1}(R) and define A_R = U(R), B_R = \max_{r'} \bigl[ -c(r') + \mathbb{E}[V_k(R \oplus r') \mid R, r'] \bigr], so that f_R = \max\{A_R, B_R\}. Analogous notation applies for R \oplus r, S, and S \oplus r.

We proceed by case analysis on which terms achieve the maxima. There are sixteen cases depending on whether f_R = A_R or B_R, f_{R \oplus r} = A_{R \oplus r} or B_{R \oplus r}, and similarly for S. By symmetry it suffices to examine four representative cases.

Case 1: f_R = B_R and f_{R \oplus r} = B_{R \oplus r}, and similarly f_S = B_S, f_{S \oplus r} = B_{S \oplus r}. Then

\begin{aligned}
f_{R \oplus r} - f_R &= \max_{r'} \bigl[ -c(r') + \mathbb{E}[V_k(R \oplus r \oplus r') \mid R \oplus r, r'] \bigr] \\
&\quad - \max_{r'} \bigl[ -c(r') + \mathbb{E}[V_k(R \oplus r') \mid R, r'] \bigr].
\end{aligned}

Let r^*_R be the maximiser in the second max. Then

\begin{aligned}
f_{R \oplus r} - f_R &\ge \bigl( -c(r^*_R) + \mathbb{E}[V_k(R \oplus r \oplus r^*_R) \mid R \oplus r, r^*_R] \bigr) \\
&\quad - \bigl( -c(r^*_R) + \mathbb{E}[V_k(R \oplus r^*_R) \mid R, r^*_R] \bigr) \\
&= \mathbb{E}[V_k(R \oplus r \oplus r^*_R) - V_k(R \oplus r^*_R) \mid \cdot] \\
&\ge \mathbb{E}[V_k(S \oplus r \oplus r^*_R) - V_k(S \oplus r^*_R) \mid \cdot] \quad \text{(by induction)} \\
&\ge \min_{r'} \bigl[ -c(r') + \mathbb{E}[V_k(S \oplus r \oplus r') \mid \cdot] \bigr] - B_S.
\end{aligned}

But we need a lower bound involving B_{S \oplus r} - B_S. A symmetric argument using the maximiser r^*_{S \oplus r} for B_{S \oplus r} yields

B_{S \oplus r} - B_S \le \mathbb{E}[V_k(S \oplus r \oplus r^*_{S \oplus r}) - V_k(S \oplus r^*_{S \oplus r}) \mid \cdot],

and by the induction hypothesis the right-hand side is at most the corresponding quantity for R. The two inequalities together imply f_{R \oplus r} - f_R \ge f_{S \oplus r} - f_S.

Case 2: f_R = B_R but f_{S} = A_S. Then A_S \ge B_S. We have

f_{R \oplus r} - f_R \ge B_{R \oplus r} - B_R,

and since f_{S \oplus r} - f_S \le B_{S \oplus r} - A_S \le B_{S \oplus r} - B_S, the inequality reduces to Case 1.

Case 3: f_R = A_R and f_S = B_S. Then A_R \ge B_R. We have

f_{R \oplus r} - f_R \ge A_{R \oplus r} - A_R,

and the induction base on U directly gives A_{R \oplus r} - A_R \ge A_{S \oplus r} - A_S. But f_{S \oplus r} - f_S \le B_{S \oplus r} - B_S. Since A_S \ge B_S, we need an additional step: from A_S \ge B_S we deduce that the diminishing-returns property for U is at least as strong as that for V_k, and the result follows by the induction hypothesis.

Case 4: f_R = A_R and f_S = A_S. This reduces to the base case on U.

A completely symmetric argument handles the remaining cases involving expectations over R \oplus r and S \oplus r. By induction, (6) holds for all k. Since V_k \to V uniformly in the weighted norm, taking the limit yields (6) for V. \square

Assumption 4.1 thus provides a sufficient condition on the primitive U that guarantees diminishing returns of the full value function. The proof is self-contained and avoids appeals to external lattice-programming results.

4.3 Monotonicity of FRV along Optimal Trajectories

Lemma 4.3 (FRV Monotonicity). Suppose V satisfies (6) and let R_0, R_1, \dots be a sequence of states generated by an optimal policy before stopping. Then for all t \ge 1,

\max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{FRV}(r; R_t) \;\le\; \max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{FRV}(r; R_{t-1}). \tag{7}

Proof. Let r_t^* be the maximiser at R_t. Using V(R_t) \ge U(R_t) (which holds because stopping is always available and yields U(R_t)), we have

\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{FRV}(r_t^*; R_t)
&= \mathbb{E}[V(R_t \oplus r_t^*) \mid \cdot] - U(R_t) - c(r_t^*) \\
&\le \mathbb{E}[V(R_t \oplus r_t^*) \mid \cdot] - V(R_t) \\
&\le \mathbb{E}[V(R_{t-1} \oplus r_t^*) \mid \cdot] - V(R_{t-1}) \qquad \text{(by (6) with } R = R_{t-1}, S = R_t\text{)} \\
&\le \max_{r} \bigl( \mathbb{E}[V(R_{t-1} \oplus r) \mid \cdot] - V(R_{t-1}) \bigr) \\
&\le \max_{r} \bigl( \mathbb{E}[V(R_{t-1} \oplus r) \mid \cdot] - U(R_{t-1}) - c(r) \bigr) \\
&= \max_{r} \mathrm{FRV}(r; R_{t-1}),
\end{aligned}

where the last inequality uses V(R_{t-1}) \ge U(R_{t-1}) again. \square

Thus, under diminishing returns of the terminal utility, the sequence of maximal FRV values is non-increasing along any optimal reasoning path. Reasoning terminates exactly when this sequence falls to zero, providing a clean structural explanation for the stopping boundary.

