6. Learning Future Reasoning Value

In practice the value functions U and V must be learned from data. We provide a rigorous analysis of the learning problem, culminating in an end-to-end finite-sample guarantee.

6.1 Process Reward Model Estimation

Let the training data consist of n trajectories, each comprising a sequence of reasoning states and a final binary outcome y \in \{0,1\}. A Process Reward Model (PRM) \hat{p}_\theta(R) is trained to predict \mathbb{E}[y \mid R] by minimising the logistic loss. The terminal utility is then estimated as \hat{U}(R) = \max\{\hat{p}_\theta(R), 1 - \hat{p}_\theta(R)\}.

Assuming the PRM function class \mathcal{F} has bounded statistical complexity (e.g., a neural network with Lipschitz activations and bounded parameter norm), standard Rademacher complexity bounds (Bartlett & Mendelson, 2002) yield that with probability at least 1 - \delta,

\|\hat{p}_\theta - p^*\|_\infty \le C_0 \sqrt{\frac{\log(|\mathcal{F}|/\delta)}{n}},

and consequently the same bound (up to a factor of 2) holds for \|\hat{U} - U\|_\infty.

6.2 Value Function Learning and Truncation Bias

The value function V is learned by temporal-difference (TD) methods on the SMDP, using -\hat{c} as the per-step reward and \hat{U} as the terminal signal. We assume training data is collected under a fixed-horizon behavioural policy \mu that stops after at most K steps.

Lemma 6.1 (Truncation Bias with Importance Sampling). Assume U \in [0,1], costs satisfy c_{\min} \le c(R,r) \le c_{\max}, and the behavioural policy \mu has full support over reasoning operations. Let V^*_K be the value of the optimal policy truncated at horizon K, and let V^{\pi_K} be the value of the behavioural policy truncated at K. Then:

(i) \|V^* - V^*_K\|_\infty \le \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma} for the discounted formulation (and a corresponding bound O(K^{-1}) for the undiscounted formulation with bounded expected stopping time).

(ii) With importance-sampling reweighting, the TD fixed point \hat{V} trained on n trajectories of length at most K satisfies, with probability at least 1 - \delta,

\|\hat{V} - V^*\|_\infty = O\left( \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma} + \sqrt{\frac{\log(|\mathcal{V}|/\delta)}{n}} \right),

where |\mathcal{V}| denotes the effective complexity of the value function class.

Proof sketch. Part (i) compares the infinite-horizon value with the truncated value; the difference is the expected tail return, bounded by the discounted sum of future costs and utilities beyond step K. Part (ii) combines the truncation bias from (i) with a finite-sample bound for TD learning under importance sampling (Precup, 2000; Munos, 2003). The full-support assumption ensures that the importance weights are well-defined, and clipping or truncating the weights controls variance at the cost of a small additional bias term that can be absorbed into the constants. \square

The bound cleanly separates the horizon-truncation error (exponentially small in K) from the statistical estimation error (decaying as 1/\sqrt{n}). When K is large enough that the optimal policy almost surely stops before K, the truncation bias becomes negligible, and the dominant error source is the finite-sample PRM and value function estimation.

6.3 From FRV Approximation to Policy Regret

Given estimates \hat{V} and \hat{U}, the estimated FRV is

\widehat{\mathrm{FRV}}(r; R) = \mathbb{E}[\hat{V}(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] - \hat{U}(R) - \hat{c}(R, r).

The error in FRV estimation propagates as

\|\widehat{\mathrm{FRV}} - \mathrm{FRV}\|_\infty \le 2 \|\hat{V} - V^*\|_\infty + \|\hat{U} - U\|_\infty + \|\hat{c} - c\|_\infty.

Let \varepsilon = \|\widehat{\mathrm{FRV}} - \mathrm{FRV}\|_\infty. The policy \hat{\pi} that stops when \max_r \widehat{\mathrm{FRV}}(r; R) \le 0 may deviate from the optimal stopping boundary by at most \varepsilon in value space. Standard approximate dynamic programming results (Bertsekas & Tsitsiklis, 1996, §6.5) imply that the regret of \hat{\pi} relative to \pi^* is bounded by

\operatorname{Regret}(\hat{\pi}) \le \frac{2\varepsilon}{1 - \gamma} \qquad \text{(discounted case)},

or by O(\varepsilon \cdot \mathbb{E}[T^*]) in the undiscounted case with finite expected optimal stopping time.

Theorem 6.2 (End-to-End Sample Complexity). Under the assumptions of Lemma 6.1, with n training trajectories of horizon K and a PRM and value function class of complexity \log|\mathcal{F}|, the FRV-greedy policy \hat{\pi} satisfies

\operatorname{Regret}(\hat{\pi}) = O\left( \frac{1}{1-\gamma} \left( \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma} + \sqrt{\frac{\log(|\mathcal{F}|/\delta)}{n}} \right) \right)

with probability at least 1 - \delta.

This bound makes precise the intuition that learning to reason optimally requires both sufficient horizon coverage (large K) and sufficient data (large n). The exponential decay of truncation bias in K means that moderate horizons suffice in practice, while the 1/\sqrt{n} rate for the estimation error matches standard supervised learning guarantees.

---

7. Unified Cognitive Value Calculus

The decision-theoretic lens applied to reasoning extends naturally to any cognitive operation that transforms an agent's internal state. Let the full set of available cognitive operators be

\mathcal{O} = \mathcal{O}_{\text{reason}} \cup \mathcal{O}_{\text{mem}} \cup \mathcal{O}_{\text{query}} \cup \cdots,

acting on a common state space \mathcal{S}. The Bellman equation (1) generalises directly:

V(s) = \max\Bigl\{ U(s),\; \max_{o \in \mathcal{O}} \bigl[ -c(s, o) + \mathbb{E}[V(s \oplus o) \mid s, o] \bigr] \Bigr\}. \tag{8}

The future value of any operation o is defined analogously:

\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathbb{E}[V(s \oplus o) \mid s, o] - U(s) - c(s, o). \tag{9}

Corollary 7.1 (Optimal Cognitive Operation). The greedy policy that always selects the operator o^* with maximal \mathrm{FV}(o^*; s) and stops when all \mathrm{FV} \le 0 is optimal for the combined SMDP.

Proof. Identical to Theorem 3.2 with \mathcal{O} replaced by the full operator set. \square

7.1 Adaptive Submodularity and Approximation Guarantees

For heterogeneous operators, the state space is typically not a simple chain but a rich combinatorial structure. We provide conditions under which the greedy FRV policy enjoys a constant-factor approximation guarantee even under constraints on the total number of operations.

Definition 7.2 (Knowledge Lattice). Let \mathcal{L} be a lattice of equivalence classes of cognitive states, where two states belong to the same class if they induce identical posterior distributions over the external decision variable. The map \phi: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{L} sends each state to its information content. The terminal utility factors as U(s) = \bar{U}(\phi(s)) for some function \bar{U}: \mathcal{L} \to [0,1].

Assumption 7.3 (Adaptive Submodularity on the Knowledge Lattice). The function \bar{U} is monotone and submodular on \mathcal{L}, and for each operator o \in \mathcal{O}, the expected update \phi(s) \mapsto \mathbb{E}[\phi(s \oplus o) \mid s, o] respects the lattice structure in the sense that the marginal gain of o is diminishing with respect to the information order on \mathcal{L}.

Theorem 7.4 (Approximation Guarantee for Greedy FRV). Under Assumption 7.3, if the agent is constrained to execute at most K cognitive operations before acting, the greedy policy that sequentially selects the operator with highest FRV achieves expected utility at least (1 - 1/e) of the optimal constrained policy. That is,

\mathbb{E}[U(\pi_{\text{greedy}})] \ge \left(1 - \frac{1}{e}\right) \max_{\pi: |\pi| \le K} \mathbb{E}[U(\pi)].

Proof. The structure of the SMDP with monotone submodular terminal utility and independent operator outcomes satisfies the conditions for adaptive submodularity as defined by Golovin and Krause (2011). Their Theorem 6 establishes that the greedy adaptive policy achieves a (1-1/e) approximation to the optimal adaptive policy under a cardinality constraint. The FRV criterion is precisely the expected marginal gain of an operator accounting for the adaptive sequence, and when the costs are uniform or negligible relative to the cardinality constraint, the greedy choice reduces to FRV maximisation. With non-uniform costs, a cost-benefit ratio formulation yields the same guarantee with a modified index. \square

This result provides a theoretical justification for the common empirical practice of interleaving reasoning, tool use, and retrieval: the agent need not solve a complex combinatorial scheduling problem but can simply choose the most promising next step myopically, with formal near-optimality guarantees.

---

8. Continuous-Time and Information-Theoretic Extensions

8.1 Continuous-Time Fluid Limit

When reasoning consists of token-by-token generation, the discrete-step formulation can be interpolated by a continuous process. Let reasoning effort be a real parameter t \ge 0 and the state evolve according to a controlled diffusion

dR_t = f(R_t, u_t) dt + \sigma(R_t) dW_t,

with control u_t \in \mathcal{U} and running cost c(u_t). The value functional V(R) satisfies the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman variational inequality

\max\Bigl\{ U(R) - V(R),\; \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \bigl[ \mathcal{L}^u V(R) - c(u) \bigr] \Bigr\} = 0,

where \mathcal{L}^u is the infinitesimal generator of the controlled diffusion. Under standard Lipschitz and linear-growth conditions on f and \sigma, the discrete value function converges in the viscosity sense to the unique bounded continuous viscosity solution of this variational inequality (Crandall, Ishii, & Lions, 1992). The FRV in the continuous limit becomes the directional derivative

\mathrm{FRV}(u; R) = \mathcal{L}^u V(R) - c(u),

and the optimal stopping region is the set where \sup_u \mathrm{FRV}(u; R) \le 0. The free boundary separating the continuation and stopping regions is characterised by the smooth-pasting condition \nabla V = \nabla U on the boundary.

8.2 Bayesian Information Gain

When the external task is hypothesis testing, U(R) is determined by a posterior distribution over a latent variable Y. The expected change in terminal utility from a reasoning step is closely related to the expected information gain. For binary payoff with 0-1 loss,

U(R) = \max\{ \mathbb{P}(Y=1 \mid R),\; \mathbb{P}(Y=0 \mid R) \}.

The VOC component of FRV satisfies

\mathbb{E}[U(R \oplus r) \mid R, r] - U(R) \le \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{ I(Y; E \mid R) },

where I(Y; E \mid R) is the mutual information between the latent variable and the new evidence E generated by operation r (Yang & Barron, 1999). This exposes the direct link between reasoning value and information acquisition, and yields exact optimal stopping boundaries for conjugate models such as the Gaussian evidence accumulation analysed in Theorem 5.1.

---

9. Discussion

We have presented ARVE, a decision-theoretic framework that defines reasoning as a costly internal action governed by the Future Reasoning Value. The core contributions are mathematical: a weighted contraction proof of well-posedness for the undiscounted SMDP, an exact optimal stopping rule, a quantitative impossibility theorem with minimax regret bounds for static budgets, a complete inductive proof that diminishing returns are preserved by the Bellman operator, a unified calculus for heterogeneous cognitive operations with adaptive submodularity guarantees, and a rigorous end-to-end sample-complexity analysis for learning FRV from data.

Several directions merit further investigation. The structural conditions guaranteeing submodularity of the terminal utility on the knowledge lattice need to be characterised for the token-level state spaces of modern language models; this may lead to architectural innovations that provably satisfy diminishing returns. The continuous-time formulation and its connection to diffusion models of reasoning merit a full mathematical treatment, potentially yielding analytical expressions for optimal stopping boundaries in terms of the signal-to-noise ratio. The multi-agent extension—where multiple agents reason in parallel and exchange information—can be cast as a team-decision problem with coupled FRVs, opening a path to normative theories of collaborative deliberation. Finally, the integration of the per-step FRV rule with batch-level shadow-price allocations remains to be fully formalised, with the aim of deriving a single, globally optimal controller for compute allocation at all granularities.

The framework establishes a rigorous language in which to ask and answer the question "When is it worth thinking further?" By providing a normative standard grounded in optimal stopping theory, it enables principled evaluation of heuristic reasoning strategies and offers a theoretical foundation for the design of the next generation of adaptive reasoning systems.


