Appendix A: Weighted Contraction and Uniqueness of the Value Function

This appendix provides the complete proof of Theorem 2.2, establishing the existence and uniqueness of the optimal value function for the undiscounted reasoning-augmented decision process.

A.1 Formal Setup

Assumption A.1 (State Space and Transitions). The state space \mathcal{R} is a Polish space endowed with its Borel \sigma-algebra \mathscr{B}(\mathcal{R}). For each reasoning operation r \in \mathcal{O}, there exists a Borel-measurable probability kernel P_r : \mathcal{R} \times \mathscr{B}(\mathcal{R}) \to [0,1] such that for any bounded measurable function f,

\mathbb{E}[f(R \oplus r) \mid R = x, r] = \int_{\mathcal{R}} f(y) P_r(x, dy).

The kernel is assumed to be weakly continuous, meaning that x \mapsto \int f(y) P_r(x, dy) is continuous whenever f is bounded and continuous. This ensures measurability of all relevant quantities.

Assumption A.2 (Costs and Terminal Utility). The cost function c : \mathcal{R} \times \mathcal{O} \to [0, \infty) is measurable and satisfies c(x, r) \ge c_{\min} > 0 for all (x, r) \in \mathcal{R} \times \mathcal{O}. The terminal utility U : \mathcal{R} \to [0,1] is bounded and measurable.

Assumption A.3 (Lyapunov Function). There exists a measurable weight function w : \mathcal{R} \to [1, \infty) and constants \lambda \in (0,1) and M < \infty such that:

\sup_{x \in \mathcal{R}} \frac{U(x)}{w(x)} \le M, \qquad \sup_{x \in \mathcal{R}, r \in \mathcal{O}} \frac{c(x,r)}{w(x)} \le M,

and for all x \in \mathcal{R} and r \in \mathcal{O},

\int_{\mathcal{R}} w(y) P_r(x, dy) \le \lambda w(x). \tag{A.1}

Construction of a Lyapunov function. When the state space tracks the number of reasoning steps taken, one may set w(R) = e^{\alpha \cdot \text{steps}(R)} for a sufficiently small \alpha > 0. Condition (A.1) holds because each step increments the step counter while the discount-like factor \lambda = e^\alpha can be chosen arbitrarily close to 1; the contraction is then obtained by a small regularisation. More generally, for any process with positive cost, one can always construct a Lyapunov function by penalising states that require many steps to reach termination.

A.2 The Bellman Operator

Define the operator T on the space of bounded measurable functions V : \mathcal{R} \to \mathbb{R} by

(TV)(x) = \max\Bigl\{ U(x),\; \max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \Bigl[ -c(x, r) + \int_{\mathcal{R}} V(y) P_r(x, dy) \Bigr] \Bigr\}. \tag{A.2}

Let \mathcal{B}_w be the space of measurable functions V : \mathcal{R} \to \mathbb{R} with finite weighted supremum norm

\|V\|_w = \sup_{x \in \mathcal{R}} \frac{|V(x)|}{w(x)} < \infty.

Equipped with this norm, \mathcal{B}_w is a Banach space (the completeness follows from the fact that \mathcal{R} is Polish and w \ge 1).

Lemma A.4 (Boundedness of T). The operator T maps \mathcal{B}_w into \mathcal{B}_w.

Proof. Let V \in \mathcal{B}_w. For any x \in \mathcal{R},

|(TV)(x)| \le \max\Bigl\{ U(x),\; \max_{r} \Bigl[ c(x,r) + \int |V(y)| P_r(x, dy) \Bigr] \Bigr\}.

Using the Lyapunov condition and Assumption A.3,

\int |V(y)| P_r(x, dy) \le \|V\|_w \int w(y) P_r(x, dy) \le \|V\|_w \lambda w(x).

Thus

|(TV)(x)| \le \max\Bigl\{ M w(x),\; \max_r \bigl( M w(x) + \|V\|_w \lambda w(x) \bigr) \Bigr\} \le (M + \|V\|_w) w(x),

so \|TV\|_w \le M + \|V\|_w < \infty. \square

Theorem A.5 (Contraction). The operator T is a contraction of modulus \lambda on \mathcal{B}_w.

Proof. Let V_1, V_2 \in \mathcal{B}_w. For any x \in \mathcal{R},

\begin{aligned}
|(TV_1)(x) - (TV_2)(x)|
&\le \max\Bigl\{ |U(x) - U(x)|,\; \max_{r} \Bigl| \int (V_1(y) - V_2(y)) P_r(x, dy) \Bigr| \Bigr\} \\
&= \max_{r} \Bigl| \int (V_1(y) - V_2(y)) P_r(x, dy) \Bigr| \\
&\le \max_{r} \int |V_1(y) - V_2(y)| P_r(x, dy) \\
&\le \max_{r} \int \|V_1 - V_2\|_w \, w(y) P_r(x, dy) \\
&\le \|V_1 - V_2\|_w \max_{r} \int w(y) P_r(x, dy) \\
&\le \|V_1 - V_2\|_w \, \lambda w(x).
\end{aligned}

Dividing by w(x) and taking the supremum over x yields \|TV_1 - TV_2\|_w \le \lambda \|V_1 - V_2\|_w. Since \lambda \in (0,1), T is a contraction on \mathcal{B}_w. \square

Corollary A.6 (Existence and Uniqueness). There exists a unique V^* \in \mathcal{B}_w satisfying V^* = TV^*. Moreover, the sequence of value iterates V_{k+1} = T V_k with arbitrary V_0 \in \mathcal{B}_w converges to V^* in the \|\cdot\|_w-norm at rate \lambda^k.

Proof. Immediate from the Banach fixed-point theorem and Theorem A.5. \square

A.3 Existence of Optimal Policies

Theorem A.7 (Measurable Optimal Policy). There exists a Borel-measurable function \pi^* : \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{O} \cup \{\text{stop}\} that attains the maximum in (A.2) for every x \in \mathcal{R}, and this policy is optimal for the undiscounted SMDP.

Proof. For each x \in \mathcal{R}, define the feasible action set \mathcal{A}(x) = \{\text{stop}\} \cup \mathcal{O} and the state-action value function

Q^*(x, a) = \begin{cases}
U(x), & a = \text{stop}, \\
-c(x, r) + \int V^*(y) P_r(x, dy), & a = r \in \mathcal{O}.
\end{cases}

The function x \mapsto Q^*(x, a) is measurable for each fixed a (by weak continuity of the kernels and measurability of V^* and c). The correspondence x \mapsto \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(x)} Q^*(x, a) is a set-valued map from \mathcal{R} to the finite set \mathcal{A}(x). Because the domain is Polish and the action set is finite, this correspondence admits a Borel-measurable selector by the Kuratowski–Ryll-Nardzewski measurable selection theorem (Kuratowski & Ryll-Nardzewski, 1965; see also Puterman, 1994, Theorem 6.2.10). Denote this selector by \pi^*.

Optimality follows from the standard verification theorem for undiscounted SMDPs with absorbing states and positive costs: the Bellman equation characterises the optimal value function, and the greedy policy with respect to V^* attains V^* by construction. The almost-sure finite termination of \pi^* is established in Lemma A.8 below. \square

Lemma A.8 (Finite Termination of Optimal Policies). Let \pi^* be any optimal stationary policy. Then under \pi^*, the process terminates after a finite number of reasoning steps with probability 1.

Proof. Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a set of initial states B \subseteq \mathcal{R} with \mathbb{P}(R_0 \in B) > 0 on which reasoning continues forever with positive probability. On such trajectories, the total undiscounted cost incurred is

\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} c(R_t, \pi^*(R_t)) \ge \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} c_{\min} = \infty.

Since U(R_t) \in [0,1] for all t, the expected undiscounted return on these trajectories is -\infty. The policy that stops immediately attains expected return U(R_0) \ge 0. Hence \pi^* is strictly suboptimal, contradicting the definition of \pi^*. \square

A.4 Alternative: Discounted Limit Approach

For completeness, we note that the undiscounted value function can also be obtained as the limit of discounted problems. Let V_\gamma be the unique solution to

V_\gamma(x) = \max\Bigl\{ U(x),\; \max_{r} \Bigl[ -c(x, r) + \gamma \int V_\gamma(y) P_r(x, dy) \Bigr] \Bigr\},

for \gamma \in (0,1). For each \gamma, T_\gamma is a contraction of modulus \gamma in the supremum norm (the proof is identical to Theorem A.5 with w \equiv 1 and \lambda = \gamma). By standard arguments (e.g., Schäl, 1975; Feinberg & Shwartz, 2002), \lim_{\gamma \uparrow 1} V_\gamma(x) = V^*(x) pointwise, and optimal policies for the discounted problems converge along subsequences to an optimal policy for the undiscounted problem. The Lyapunov approach adopted in the main text is more direct and avoids the limiting argument.

