Appendix B: Optimal Stopping Rule — Complete Derivation

This appendix provides the full derivation of Theorem 3.2, including the precise relationship between FRV and the Bellman optimality condition.

B.1 Undiscounted Formulation

Throughout this appendix we work with the undiscounted Bellman equation (1) and the Lyapunov-weighted formulation of Appendix A. All results are stated for the unique optimal value function V^* established in Theorem A.5.

Definition B.1 (Future Reasoning Value — Undiscounted). For state x \in \mathcal{R} and operation r \in \mathcal{O},

\mathrm{FRV}(r; x) = \int_{\mathcal{R}} V^*(y) P_r(x, dy) - U(x) - c(x, r). \tag{B.1}

Theorem B.2 (Optimal Stopping Rule). The optimal policy \pi^* satisfies

\pi^*(x) = \begin{cases}
\text{stop} & \text{if } \displaystyle\max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{FRV}(r; x) \le 0, \\
\text{continue with } r^* = \arg\max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{FRV}(r; x) & \text{if } \displaystyle\max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{FRV}(r; x) > 0.
\end{cases} \tag{B.2}

Proof. From the Bellman equation (A.2),

V^*(x) = \max\Bigl\{ U(x),\; \max_{r} \Bigl[ -c(x, r) + \int V^*(y) P_r(x, dy) \Bigr] \Bigr\}.

The stopping condition V^*(x) = U(x) holds if and only if

\max_{r} \Bigl[ -c(x, r) + \int V^*(y) P_r(x, dy) \Bigr] \le U(x).

Subtracting U(x) from both sides yields

\max_{r} \Bigl[ \int V^*(y) P_r(x, dy) - U(x) - c(x, r) \Bigr] \le 0,

which is exactly \max_r \mathrm{FRV}(r; x) \le 0.

When \max_r \mathrm{FRV}(r; x) > 0, the maximum in the Bellman equation is attained by the reasoning operation that maximises the bracketed expression. Since

\arg\max_{r} \Bigl[ -c(x, r) + \int V^*(y) P_r(x, dy) \Bigr] = \arg\max_{r} \mathrm{FRV}(r; x),

the greedy choice in (B.2) coincides with the Bellman optimal action. \square

B.2 Relationship to the Classical Value of Computation

Russell and Wefald (1991) define the Value of Computation (VOC) for a single computation \psi given belief state b as

\mathrm{VOC}(\psi; b) = \mathbb{E}_{b}[U(b \circ \psi)] - U(b) - \text{cost}(\psi),

where b \circ \psi denotes the belief state after executing \psi. VOC measures the immediate improvement in decision quality without accounting for the possibility of further computation.

Proposition B.3 (FRV-VOC Decomposition). For any state x \in \mathcal{R} and operation r \in \mathcal{O},

\mathrm{FRV}(r; x) = \underbrace{\Bigl( \int U(y) P_r(x, dy) - U(x) - c(x, r) \Bigr)}_{\mathrm{VOC}(r; x)} + \underbrace{\int \bigl( V^*(y) - U(y) \bigr) P_r(x, dy)}_{\text{continuation value}}. \tag{B.3}

Proof. Add and subtract \int U(y) P_r(x, dy) in the definition of FRV:

\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{FRV}(r; x) &= \int V^*(y) P_r(x, dy) - U(x) - c(x, r) \\
&= \Bigl( \int U(y) P_r(x, dy) - U(x) - c(x, r) \Bigr) \\
&\quad + \int \bigl( V^*(y) - U(y) \bigr) P_r(x, dy).
\end{aligned}

The first bracketed term is precisely the VOC of operation r from state x. The second term is the expected continuation value: V^*(y) - U(y) \ge 0 quantifies the additional value attainable by further reasoning beyond state y. \square

The continuation value is non-negative because the agent can always choose to stop immediately and receive U(y); the Bellman equation guarantees V^*(y) \ge U(y) for all y. The decomposition reveals that FRV generalises VOC by incorporating the option value of future reasoning—an operation with negative immediate VOC may still have positive FRV if it enables subsequent reasoning steps that yield high terminal utility.

B.3 Discounted Formulation (Remarks)

For problems where the external environment itself involves temporal discounting with factor \gamma \in (0,1), one may work with the discounted Bellman equation

V_\gamma(x) = \max\Bigl\{ U(x),\; \max_{r} \Bigl[ -c(x, r) + \gamma \int V_\gamma(y) P_r(x, dy) \Bigr] \Bigr\}.

The discounted FRV is then defined as

\mathrm{FRV}_\gamma(r; x) = \gamma \int V_\gamma(y) P_r(x, dy) - U(x) - c(x, r) + (1-\gamma) U(x).

In the limit \gamma \uparrow 1, \mathrm{FRV}_\gamma converges to the undiscounted FRV, and the optimal stopping rule takes the same threshold form. The undiscounted formulation is preferred for pure reasoning tasks where the external action is taken exactly once at termination, and temporal discounting is irrelevant.

