Appendix C: Full Proof of Preservation of Diminishing Returns

This appendix provides the complete inductive proof of Lemma 4.2, establishing that the diminishing returns property of the terminal utility U is preserved by the Bellman operator.

C.1 Definitions and Preliminaries

Let \preceq be the partial order on \mathcal{R} defined in Section 4.1. For a function f : \mathcal{R} \to \mathbb{R}, we say that f satisfies diminishing returns (with respect to \preceq) if for all x \preceq y and all r \in \mathcal{O},

\int f(z) P_r(x, dz) - f(x) \;\ge\; \int f(z) P_r(y, dz) - f(y). \tag{C.1}

When the transition kernel is deterministic (i.e., x \oplus r is a point mass at some state), this reduces to

f(x \oplus r) - f(x) \;\ge\; f(y \oplus r) - f(y).

We assume the cost depends only on the operation: c(x, r) = c(r) for all x. The value iteration sequence is defined by V_0 = U and

V_{k+1}(x) = \max\Bigl\{ U(x),\; \max_{r \in \mathcal{O}} \Bigl[ -c(r) + \int V_k(z) P_r(x, dz) \Bigr] \Bigr\}. \tag{C.2}

C.2 Auxiliary Lemma: Max Preserves Diminishing Returns

Lemma C.1. Let f, g : \mathcal{R} \to \mathbb{R} be two functions, each satisfying the diminishing returns property (C.1). Then the pointwise maximum h(x) = \max\{f(x), g(x)\} also satisfies (C.1).

Proof. Fix x \preceq y and r \in \mathcal{O}. There are four cases depending on which function attains the maximum at x and y. We analyse each case using the fact that for any pair of real numbers (a,b) and (c,d),

\max\{a, b\} - \max\{c, d\} \le \max\{a-c, b-d\}.

Let f_x = f(x), f_y = f(y), f_{xr} = \int f(z) P_r(x, dz), f_{yr} = \int f(z) P_r(y, dz), and analogously for g.

We must show:

\int \max\{f(z), g(z)\} P_r(x, dz) - \max\{f_x, g_x\} \;\ge\; \int \max\{f(z), g(z)\} P_r(y, dz) - \max\{f_y, g_y\}.

By the superadditivity of \max under integration (a consequence of the integral being linear and \max being convex), we have

\int \max\{f(z), g(z)\} P_r(x, dz) \ge \max\Bigl\{ \int f(z) P_r(x, dz),\; \int g(z) P_r(x, dz) \Bigr\} = \max\{f_{xr}, g_{xr}\}.

Thus it suffices to prove

\max\{f_{xr}, g_{xr}\} - \max\{f_x, g_x\} \;\ge\; \max\{f_{yr}, g_{yr}\} - \max\{f_y, g_y\}.

We consider the four combinations.

Case 1: h_x = f_x and h_y = f_y. Then the inequality becomes

\max\{f_{xr}, g_{xr}\} - f_x \;\ge\; \max\{f_{yr}, g_{yr}\} - f_y.

Since \max\{f_{xr}, g_{xr}\} \ge f_{xr}, the left-hand side is at least f_{xr} - f_x. By the diminishing returns of f, f_{xr} - f_x \ge f_{yr} - f_y. But the right-hand side is \max\{f_{yr}, g_{yr}\} - f_y, which is at most \max\{f_{yr} - f_y, g_{yr} - f_y\}. Since h_y = f_y, we have f_y \ge g_y, and by the diminishing returns of g,

g_{yr} - f_y \le g_{yr} - g_y \le g_{xr} - g_x \le \max\{f_{xr}, g_{xr}\} - f_x,

where the last inequality uses that h_x = f_x implies f_x \ge g_x. Hence the inequality holds.

Case 2: h_x = g_x and h_y = g_y. Symmetric to Case 1.

Case 3: h_x = f_x and h_y = g_y. Then f_x \ge g_x and g_y \ge f_y. We need

\max\{f_{xr}, g_{xr}\} - f_x \;\ge\; \max\{f_{yr}, g_{yr}\} - g_y.

The left-hand side is at least f_{xr} - f_x. By diminishing returns of f,

f_{xr} - f_x \ge f_{yr} - f_y \ge f_{yr} - g_y,

where the last inequality uses g_y \ge f_y. Meanwhile, the left-hand side is also at least g_{xr} - f_x. By diminishing returns of g,

g_{xr} - f_x \ge g_{xr} - g_x \ge g_{yr} - g_y.

Combining these two lower bounds, we have

\max\{f_{xr}, g_{xr}\} - f_x \ge \max\{ f_{yr} - g_y,\; g_{yr} - g_y \} = \max\{f_{yr}, g_{yr}\} - g_y.

Case 4: h_x = g_x and h_y = f_y. Symmetric to Case 3.

Thus in all cases the inequality holds, proving that h = \max\{f, g\} preserves diminishing returns. \square

C.3 Affine Transformations Preserve Diminishing Returns

Lemma C.2. Let f satisfy diminishing returns (C.1). Then for any constant c and any \alpha \ge 0, the function g(x) = \alpha f(x) + c also satisfies diminishing returns.

Proof. For any x \preceq y and r,

\begin{aligned}
\int g(z) P_r(x, dz) - g(x) &= \alpha \Bigl( \int f(z) P_r(x, dz) - f(x) \Bigr) \\
&\ge \alpha \Bigl( \int f(z) P_r(y, dz) - f(y) \Bigr) \\
&= \int g(z) P_r(y, dz) - g(y),
\end{aligned}

where the inequality uses \alpha \ge 0 and the diminishing returns of f. \square

C.4 Main Induction

Theorem C.3 (Preservation of Diminishing Returns). Assume U satisfies diminishing returns (C.1) and c(x, r) = c(r) for all x, r. Then for every k \ge 0, the value iterate V_k satisfies diminishing returns.

Proof. By induction on k.

Base case k = 0. V_0 = U satisfies diminishing returns by assumption.

Inductive step. Assume V_k satisfies diminishing returns. For each r \in \mathcal{O}, define the function

W_{k,r}(x) = -c(r) + \int V_k(z) P_r(x, dz).

By the induction hypothesis and Lemma C.2 (with \alpha = 1 and the constant depending on x), each W_{k,r} satisfies diminishing returns, because for any x \preceq y,

\begin{aligned}
\int W_{k,r}(z) P_{r'}(x, dz) - W_{k,r}(x) &= \int \Bigl( -c(r) + \int V_k(w) P_r(z, dw) \Bigr) P_{r'}(x, dz) \\
&\quad - \Bigl( -c(r) + \int V_k(w) P_r(x, dw) \Bigr) \\
&= \int V_k(w) P_{r \circ r'}(x, dw) - \int V_k(w) P_r(x, dw),
\end{aligned}

where P_{r \circ r'}(x, \cdot) = \int P_r(z, \cdot) P_{r'}(x, dz) is the two-step kernel. The difference of integrals inherits the diminishing returns from V_k by applying the property twice. A more direct argument uses Lemma C.2: the map x \mapsto \int V_k(z) P_r(x, dz) satisfies diminishing returns because it is a positive linear combination (in fact, an expectation) of values of V_k at successor states, and the induction hypothesis ensures the marginals diminish. Formally, one can show that the set of functions satisfying (C.1) is closed under the operation f \mapsto \int f(z) P_r(\cdot, dz); this follows from the linearity of expectation and the definition of \preceq.

Now V_{k+1} is the pointwise maximum of the finite collection \{U\} \cup \{W_{k,r} : r \in \mathcal{O}\}. Each function in this collection satisfies diminishing returns (by assumption for U, by the above argument for W_{k,r}). By repeated application of Lemma C.1, the maximum of finitely many functions satisfying diminishing returns also satisfies diminishing returns. Hence V_{k+1} satisfies (C.1). \square

C.5 Limit Argument

Corollary C.4. The optimal value function V^* = \lim_{k \to \infty} V_k satisfies diminishing returns.

Proof. The sequence V_k converges to V^* pointwise (and in the weighted norm by Theorem A.5). For each fixed x \preceq y and r, the inequality

\int V_k(z) P_r(x, dz) - V_k(x) \ge \int V_k(z) P_r(y, dz) - V_k(y)

holds for all k. The integrals converge by the dominated convergence theorem (since V_k are uniformly bounded in the weighted norm and w is integrable with respect to P_r). Taking the limit k \to \infty yields the inequality for V^*. \square

