Appendix D: Proof of the Off-Policy Truncation Bias Bound

This appendix provides the rigorous derivation of Lemma 6.1, bounding the error in value function estimation from fixed-horizon training data.

D.1 Notation and Setup

We work with the discounted formulation (discount factor \gamma \in (0,1)) for clarity; the undiscounted bounds follow by taking \gamma \uparrow 1 under the finite-expected-stopping-time condition.

Let \pi^* be an optimal stationary policy for the infinite-horizon SMDP, with value function V^*. Let \pi_K be a fixed-horizon policy that follows a behavioural policy \mu for steps 0, 1, \dots, K-1 and then stops at step K. Let V^{\pi_K} be the true value function of \pi_K.

Let \hat{V} be the TD fixed point learned from n trajectories generated by \pi_K, using importance sampling to correct for the mismatch between \mu and the target policy. We assume the value function is approximated within a class \mathcal{V} of bounded complexity (e.g., linear function approximation with d basis functions, or a neural network with bounded Rademacher complexity \mathfrak{R}_n(\mathcal{V})).

D.2 Truncation Bias

Lemma D.1 (Truncation Error). For the optimal value function V^* and the optimal policy truncated at horizon K (denoted V^*_K),

\|V^* - V^*_K\|_\infty \le \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma}.

Proof. Let \tau^* be the optimal stopping time. The value of the truncated policy is

V^*_K(x) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi^*} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^{\min(\tau^*, K)-1} (-\gamma^t c) + \gamma^{\min(\tau^*, K)} U(R_{\min(\tau^*, K)}) \;\Big|\; R_0 = x \right].

The difference V^*(x) - V^*_K(x) is exactly the expected tail return when \tau^* > K:

V^*(x) - V^*_K(x) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi^*} \left[ \mathbf{1}_{\{\tau^* > K\}} \Bigl( \sum_{t=K}^{\tau^*-1} (-\gamma^t c) + \gamma^{\tau^*} U(R_{\tau^*}) - \gamma^K U(R_K) \Bigr) \;\Big|\; R_0 = x \right].

Since U \in [0,1] and c \ge 0, the tail return from step K onward is bounded in absolute value by \sum_{t=K}^{\infty} \gamma^t = \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma}. Hence

|V^*(x) - V^*_K(x)| \le \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma} \cdot \mathbb{P}_{\pi^*}(\tau^* > K \mid R_0 = x) \le \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma}.

Taking the supremum over x yields the bound. \square

D.3 Policy Mismatch and Importance Sampling

Let the behavioural policy \mu(a \mid x) be the probability of selecting action a (including "stop") in state x. We assume \mu(a \mid x) > 0 for all feasible actions, ensuring full support. The target policy \pi(a \mid x) is the greedy policy with respect to \hat{V}.

For a trajectory \tau = (x_0, a_0, x_1, a_1, \dots, x_T) of length T \le K, the importance-sampling weight is

\rho_{0:t} = \prod_{i=0}^{t-1} \frac{\pi(a_i \mid x_i)}{\mu(a_i \mid x_i)}.

The TD(0) update with importance sampling at step t uses the target

y_t = -c(x_t, a_t) + \gamma \hat{V}(x_{t+1}),

weighted by \rho_{0:t}. The fixed point of this weighted TD procedure, under standard coverage and contraction assumptions, converges to V^* as n \to \infty and K \to \infty.

D.4 Finite-Sample Bound

Theorem D.2 (High-Probability Error Bound). Let \mathcal{V} be a class of value functions with VC-dimension or Rademacher complexity bounded by d. Let the importance weights be clipped at some maximum value W_{\max}. Then with probability at least 1 - \delta over the n training trajectories,

\|\hat{V} - V^*\|_\infty \le \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma} + C \cdot W_{\max} \sqrt{\frac{d + \log(1/\delta)}{n}} + \text{bias}(W_{\max}),

where \text{bias}(W_{\max}) = O(1/W_{\max}) is the bias introduced by weight clipping, and C is an absolute constant.

Proof sketch. Decompose the error into three terms:

\|\hat{V} - V^*\|_\infty \le \underbrace{\|V^* - V^*_K\|_\infty}_{\text{truncation bias}} + \underbrace{\|V^*_K - V^{\pi_K}\|_\infty}_{\text{policy mismatch}} + \underbrace{\|\hat{V} - V^{\pi_K}\|_\infty}_{\text{estimation error}}.

The truncation bias is bounded by Lemma D.1. The policy mismatch term is bounded by the total variation distance between \pi^* and \pi_K on the first K steps; under full support of \mu, importance sampling ensures that the mismatch can be corrected in expectation, and the residual bias after clipping is O(1/W_{\max}) (see Munos, 2003; Dudík et al., 2011).

The estimation error \|\hat{V} - V^{\pi_K}\|_\infty is the error of TD learning from finite data under the behavioural policy. Standard results for off-policy TD (e.g., Bhandari et al., 2018; Fan et al., 2020) yield a bound of order W_{\max} \sqrt{d/n} with high probability, where d is the effective dimension of the function class. The bound accounts for the variance inflation due to importance weights (the factor W_{\max}) and the statistical complexity of \mathcal{V}.

Balancing the clipping bias and the variance term by setting W_{\max} \asymp \sqrt{n} (or using adaptive clipping schemes) yields the overall rate O(\gamma^K/(1-\gamma) + \sqrt{d/n}). \square

Remark D.3. For the undiscounted formulation with finite expected optimal stopping time \mathbb{E}[T^*] < \infty, the truncation bias is O(1/K) rather than O(\gamma^K), and the discounted bound above adapts by sending \gamma \uparrow 1 appropriately.

