Appendix E: Adaptive Submodularity and the (1-1/e) Guarantee

This appendix develops the formal connection between the unified cognitive calculus of Section 7 and the theory of adaptive submodular maximisation.

E.1 Knowledge Lattice and Operator Outcomes

Definition E.1 (Information Map). Let \Theta be the space of possible external decision variables (e.g., the correct answer to a question). The agent's state s \in \mathcal{S} induces a posterior distribution \mathbb{P}(\theta \mid s) over \Theta. Define the information map \phi : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{P}(\Theta), where \mathcal{P}(\Theta) is the space of probability measures on \Theta. Two states s, s' are informationally equivalent if \phi(s) = \phi(s').

Definition E.2 (Knowledge Lattice). Let \mathcal{L} = \phi(\mathcal{S}) be the set of realisable posterior distributions. We equip \mathcal{L} with the partial order of statistical informativeness: \mu \preceq_{\mathcal{L}} \nu if \nu is a mean-preserving spread of \mu (Blackwell, 1953), or equivalently, if the agent with posterior \nu can simulate the agent with posterior \mu by randomising. The terminal utility factors through \phi: there exists \bar{U} : \mathcal{L} \to [0,1] such that U(s) = \bar{U}(\phi(s)).

E.2 Adaptive Submodularity

Definition E.3 (Marginal Gain). For an operator o \in \mathcal{O} and state s \in \mathcal{S}, the expected marginal gain in terminal utility is

\Delta(o \mid s) = \mathbb{E}[U(s \oplus o) \mid s, o] - U(s).

For a set of operators A \subseteq \mathcal{O}, let \Delta(A \mid s) denote the expected gain from executing all operators in A (in some order, with the expectation taken over stochastic outcomes).

Definition E.4 (Adaptive Submodularity). The pair (\mathcal{L}, \{\text{outcomes of } o\}_{o \in \mathcal{O}}) satisfies adaptive submodularity if for any two states s \preceq s' (i.e., \phi(s) \preceq_{\mathcal{L}} \phi(s')), and for any operator o \in \mathcal{O},

\Delta(o \mid s) \ge \Delta(o \mid s'). \tag{E.1}

Moreover, for any set of operators A \subseteq \mathcal{O} and any o \in \mathcal{O},

\Delta(o \mid s \oplus A) \le \Delta(o \mid s), \tag{E.2}

where s \oplus A denotes the (random) state after executing the operators in A. Condition (E.1) is precisely the diminishing returns property on the knowledge lattice, and condition (E.2) is the standard adaptive submodularity condition (Golovin & Krause, 2011, Definition 3).

Proposition E.5. If the terminal utility \bar{U} on \mathcal{L} is monotone and submodular with respect to the information order, and the operators' outcomes are conditionally independent given the latent variable \theta, then the adaptive submodularity conditions (E.1)–(E.2) hold.

Proof. Under conditional independence, the information gain from a set of operators is the sum of individual information gains (modulo the lattice structure). Submodularity of \bar{U} on \mathcal{L} implies that the marginal gain of adding an operator's outcome diminishes as the current posterior is more informative. The formal reduction follows from Golovin and Krause (2011, §6.2) and the classical results of Blackwell (1953) on comparison of experiments. \square

E.3 Greedy Approximation Guarantee

Theorem E.6 (Greedy FRV is Near-Optimal). Assume the conditions of Proposition E.5 hold, and let the agent be constrained to execute at most K cognitive operations before acting. Let \Pi_K be the set of adaptive policies that select at most K operators. Let \pi_{\text{greedy}} be the policy that, at each step, selects the operator maximising \Delta(o \mid s) - c(o) and stops when this quantity is non-positive (or after K steps, whichever comes first). Then

\mathbb{E}[U(\pi_{\text{greedy}})] \ge \left(1 - \frac{1}{e}\right) \max_{\pi \in \Pi_K} \mathbb{E}[U(\pi)] - K c_{\max}.

Proof. This is essentially a restatement of Golovin and Krause (2011, Theorem 6) adapted to our setting. The expected utility objective with per-step costs is equivalent to a pure utility maximisation problem on an augmented state space where the "value" of stopping after t steps is U(s_t) - \sum_{i=0}^{t-1} c(o_i). The greedy algorithm that maximises the cost-adjusted marginal gain \Delta(o \mid s) - c(o) attains the (1-1/e) approximation bound for adaptive submodular maximisation with a cardinality constraint, provided the objective is monotone and adaptive submodular. The subtractive cost term K c_{\max} accounts for the worst-case total cost of K operations. \square

Remark E.7. When costs are negligible compared to utility differences, the bound is tight (Feige, 1998). When costs are significant, a modified index \Delta(o \mid s) / c(o) (the "bang-per-buck") can be used to obtain a bi-criterion approximation, but the (1-1/e) bound on utility alone no longer holds in general.

