Appendix F: Convergence of the Adaptive Threshold Controller

This appendix provides a rigorous convergence analysis for the adaptive threshold controller described in Section 2.5 of the main text (now Section 6 in the revised manuscript).

F.1 Controller Dynamics

The agent maintains a threshold \tau_t \in \mathbb{R} at episode t. At each reasoning step within an episode, the agent computes the estimated maximal FRV, \hat{m}_t = \max_r \widehat{\mathrm{FRV}}_t(r; R), and continues reasoning if and only if \hat{m}_t > \tau_t. After the episode terminates, the loss is

L(\tau) = (1 - U(R_{\text{stop}})) + \lambda \sum_{\text{steps}} c_i,

where U(R_{\text{stop}}) is the terminal utility attained. The threshold is updated via stochastic gradient descent:

\tau_{t+1} = \tau_t - \eta_t \frac{\partial L}{\partial \tau},

with the derivative estimated by the REINFORCE estimator

\frac{\partial L}{\partial \tau} \approx (L - \bar{L}_t) \cdot \frac{\partial \log p_{\tau}(\text{stop} \mid \hat{m}_t)}{\partial \tau},

where p_{\tau}(\text{stop} \mid m) = \sigma(m - \tau) is the sigmoid stopping probability and \bar{L}_t is a baseline (e.g., a moving average of recent losses).

F.2 Mean-Field ODE

We study the convergence of \tau_t using the ODE method for stochastic approximation (Kushner & Yin, 2003). Assume that the FRV estimates converge to the true FRV (or are unbiased with bounded variance), and that the environment is stationary in the sense that the distribution of \hat{m}_t under the threshold policy depends smoothly on \tau.

Define the mean-field function

h(\tau) = \mathbb{E}\bigl[ \max_r \mathrm{FRV}(r; R_\tau) \bigr] - \tau,

where R_\tau is the random state at which the agent stops under threshold \tau (assuming the threshold is fixed across episodes). The expected update satisfies

\mathbb{E}[\Delta \tau_t \mid \tau_t = \tau] = -\eta_t \bigl( h(\tau) + o(1) \bigr).

The ODE limit is

\dot{\tau} = -h(\tau). \tag{F.1}

F.3 Properties of h and Convergence

Lemma F.1 (Monotonicity of h). Under the diminishing returns assumption (Lemma 4.2), the function h(\tau) is strictly decreasing on its support.

Proof. As \tau increases, the agent stops earlier, at states where the maximal FRV is smaller. By the monotonicity of FRV along trajectories (Lemma 4.3), the expected maximal FRV at the stopping state is a non-increasing function of \tau. Hence \mathbb{E}[\max_r \mathrm{FRV}(r; R_\tau)] is non-increasing in \tau, and h(\tau) = \mathbb{E}[\cdots] - \tau is strictly decreasing because of the subtractive term -\tau. \square

Theorem F.2 (Almost-Sure Convergence). Assume:

1. Step sizes satisfy \sum \eta_t = \infty, \sum \eta_t^2 < \infty.
2. The FRV estimates are unbiased with bounded variance.
3. The mean-field function h is Lipschitz continuous.
   Then \tau_t \to \tau^* almost surely, where \tau^* is the unique root of h(\tau^*) = 0.

Proof. The conditions are standard for the Robbins-Monro stochastic approximation algorithm. The decreasing property of h ensures the ODE (F.1) has a unique globally asymptotically stable equilibrium at \tau^*. Standard results (Kushner & Yin, 2003, Theorem 2.1) then imply almost-sure convergence. \square

The equilibrium \tau^* has a natural economic interpretation: it equates the expected marginal benefit of an additional reasoning step to its marginal cost. At the optimal threshold, the agent reasons until the expected FRV falls to zero, realising the normative prescription of Theorem 3.2.

