Appendix G: Continuous-Time Limit — Viscosity Solution

This appendix sketches the rigorous connection between the discrete FRV and the continuous-time Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman variational inequality.

G.1 Setup

Let the reasoning effort be a continuous parameter t \ge 0. The state evolves according to the controlled stochastic differential equation

dR_t = f(R_t, u_t) dt + \sigma(R_t) dW_t,

where u_t \in \mathcal{U} is a control process adapted to the filtration generated by the Brownian motion W_t. The agent incurs a running cost c(u_t) \ge c_{\min} > 0 and may stop at any time \tau to receive terminal reward U(R_\tau). The value function is

V(x) = \sup_{\tau, u} \mathbb{E} \left[ \int_0^\tau -c(u_t) dt + U(R_\tau) \;\Big|\; R_0 = x \right].

G.2 HJB Variational Inequality

Under standard Lipschitz and linear-growth conditions on f and \sigma, the value function V is the unique bounded continuous viscosity solution to the variational inequality

\max\Bigl\{ U(x) - V(x),\; \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \bigl[ \mathcal{L}^u V(x) - c(u) \bigr] \Bigr\} = 0, \tag{G.1}

where \mathcal{L}^u is the second-order differential operator

\mathcal{L}^u V(x) = f(x, u) \cdot \nabla V(x) + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{tr}\bigl( \sigma(x) \sigma(x)^\top \nabla^2 V(x) \bigr).

The continuation region is \mathcal{C} = \{x : V(x) > U(x)\}, and the stopping region is \mathcal{S} = \{x : V(x) = U(x)\}. The free boundary \partial \mathcal{C} \cap \partial \mathcal{S} is characterised by the smooth-pasting condition \nabla V = \nabla U (under sufficient regularity).

G.3 Discrete-to-Continuous Convergence

Consider a sequence of discrete-time problems where the time step is \Delta t = \varepsilon, the state transition is the Euler-Maruyama discretisation of the SDE, and the cost per step is c(u) \varepsilon. Let V^\varepsilon be the discrete value function. Under the standard theory of viscosity solutions (Crandall, Ishii, & Lions, 1992; Fleming & Soner, 2006), V^\varepsilon converges locally uniformly to V as \varepsilon \to 0.

The discrete FRV for control u is

\mathrm{FRV}^\varepsilon(u; x) = \mathbb{E}[V^\varepsilon(x \oplus u)] - U(x) - c(u) \varepsilon.

Expanding V^\varepsilon formally to first order,

\mathbb{E}[V^\varepsilon(x \oplus u)] \approx V^\varepsilon(x) + \mathcal{L}^u V^\varepsilon(x) \varepsilon,

so that

\mathrm{FRV}^\varepsilon(u; x) \approx \bigl( \mathcal{L}^u V^\varepsilon(x) - c(u) \bigr) \varepsilon + \bigl( V^\varepsilon(x) - U(x) \bigr).

In the continuation region where V^\varepsilon(x) > U(x), the dominant term is the first one, and the optimality condition \max_u \mathrm{FRV}^\varepsilon(u; x) > 0 corresponds to \sup_u (\mathcal{L}^u V^\varepsilon(x) - c(u)) > 0. In the limit \varepsilon \to 0, this recovers the continuous FRV as the directional derivative

\mathrm{FRV}(u; x) = \mathcal{L}^u V(x) - c(u),

and the optimal stopping rule \max_u \mathrm{FRV}(u; x) \le 0 coincides with the HJB condition for stopping.

