Appendix H: Measurable Selection of Optimal Policies

Theorem H.1 (Existence of Measurable Optimal Policy). Under the assumptions of Appendix A, there exists a Borel-measurable function \pi^* : \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{O} \cup \{\text{stop}\} that is optimal.

Proof. The action set \mathcal{A} = \mathcal{O} \cup \{\text{stop}\} is a finite set. For each a \in \mathcal{A}, the state-action value function

Q^*(x, a) = \begin{cases}
U(x), & a = \text{stop}, \\
-c(x, r) + \int_{\mathcal{R}} V^*(y) P_r(x, dy), & a = r \in \mathcal{O},
\end{cases}

is a measurable function of x (by the measurability of U, c, and V^*, and the weak continuity of the kernels P_r). Define the correspondence \Gamma : \mathcal{R} \twoheadrightarrow \mathcal{A} by

\Gamma(x) = \bigl\{ a \in \mathcal{A} : Q^*(x, a) = \max_{a' \in \mathcal{A}} Q^*(x, a') \bigr\}.

Since \mathcal{A} is finite and each Q^*(x, a) is measurable, the set \Gamma(x) is non-empty for each x, and the graph of \Gamma is a measurable subset of \mathcal{R} \times \mathcal{A}. By the Kuratowski–Ryll-Nardzewski measurable selection theorem (Kuratowski & Ryll-Nardzewski, 1965, Theorem 1), there exists a Borel-measurable selector \pi^* : \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{A} such that \pi^*(x) \in \Gamma(x) for all x \in \mathcal{R}. This selector is an optimal policy by construction, as it attains the maximum in the Bellman equation. \square

