Appendix I: Additional Lemmas and Technical Results

I.1 Boundedness of the Value Function

Lemma I.1. The optimal value function satisfies 0 \le V^*(x) \le 1 for all x \in \mathcal{R} in the undiscounted formulation.

Proof. The terminal utility satisfies U(x) \in [0,1]. Reasoning steps incur non-negative costs but may increase the terminal utility. The agent can always choose to stop immediately, so V^*(x) \ge U(x) \ge 0. For the upper bound, note that even if reasoning eventually yields perfect information (utility 1), the costs incurred along the way can only reduce the net value. Formally, for any policy \pi,

\mathbb{E} \left[ U(R_\tau) - \sum_{t=0}^{\tau-1} c(R_t, a_t) \right] \le \mathbb{E}[U(R_\tau)] \le 1,

since costs are non-negative. Hence V^*(x) \le 1. \square

I.2 Monotonicity of the Stopping Region

Lemma I.2. If the terminal utility satisfies the diminishing returns property (Assumption 4.1), then the stopping region \mathcal{S} = \{x : \max_r \mathrm{FRV}(r; x) \le 0\} is absorbing under the optimal policy: if x \in \mathcal{S} and x' is reachable from x via any sequence of reasoning operations, then x' \in \mathcal{S}.

Proof. From Lemma 4.3, the maximal FRV is non-increasing along optimal trajectories. If at some state x the maximum FRV is \le 0, then at any subsequent state reachable under the optimal policy (which continues only when FRV > 0), the maximum FRV is also \le 0. Hence \mathcal{S} is closed under optimal transitions. Moreover, the diminishing returns property ensures that even if a suboptimal deviation temporarily increases FRV, the agent would immediately stop afterwards. \square

I.3 Equivalence of Discounted and Undiscounted Limits

Lemma I.3. Let V_\gamma be the optimal value function for the discounted problem with discount factor \gamma \in (0,1). Then for all x \in \mathcal{R},

\lim_{\gamma \uparrow 1} V_\gamma(x) = V^*(x),

where V^* is the undiscounted optimal value function from Theorem A.5.

Proof. This is a standard result for SMDPs with positive costs and bounded rewards (Schäl, 1975; Feinberg & Shwartz, 2002). The key step is to note that the family \{V_\gamma\}_{\gamma \in (0,1)} is uniformly bounded (by Lemma I.1) and equicontinuous in the weighted norm. The limit \gamma \uparrow 1 of the discounted optimality equations yields the undiscounted Bellman equation, and the limit value function is the unique solution. \square

