Appendix K: End-to-End Sample Complexity — Complete Derivation

This appendix assembles the bounds from Appendices D and J into a unified finite-sample guarantee.

Theorem K.1 (End-to-End Sample Complexity). Assume the conditions of Lemma 6.1 and Lemma D.1. Let the PRM be trained on n trajectories of horizon K, and let the value function be learned by TD with importance sampling as in Appendix D. Let \hat{\pi} be the greedy policy with respect to the estimated FRV. Then with probability at least 1 - \delta,

\operatorname{Regret}(\hat{\pi}) = O\left( \frac{1}{1-\gamma} \left( \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma} + W_{\max} \sqrt{\frac{d + \log(1/\delta)}{n}} \right) + \frac{1}{W_{\max}} \right),

where d is the effective dimension of the function class and W_{\max} is the importance-weight clipping threshold.

Proof. The error in the estimated FRV is, by the triangle inequality and the bounds of Appendices D and J,

\|\widehat{\mathrm{FRV}} - \mathrm{FRV}\|_\infty \le 2 \|\hat{V} - V^*\|_\infty + \|\hat{U} - U\|_\infty =: \varepsilon.

From Theorem D.2 and the PRM estimation bound (Section 6.1),

\varepsilon = O\left( \frac{\gamma^K}{1-\gamma} + W_{\max} \sqrt{\frac{d + \log(1/\delta)}{n}} + \frac{1}{W_{\max}} \right).

Standard approximate dynamic programming (Bertsekas & Tsitsiklis, 1996, Proposition 6.1) shows that an \varepsilon-approximate value function yields an O(\varepsilon/(1-\gamma)) bound on the regret of the greedy policy. Substituting the expression for \varepsilon and optimising over W_{\max} (choosing W_{\max} \asymp n^{1/3} to balance variance and clipping bias) yields the claimed rate. \square

Remark K.2. For the undiscounted case with finite expected stopping time \mathbb{E}[T^*] < \infty, the discount factor (1-\gamma)^{-1} is replaced by \mathbb{E}[T^*], and the truncation bias \gamma^K/(1-\gamma) becomes \mathbb{P}(T^* > K) \cdot \mathbb{E}[T^* - K \mid T^* > K], which decays as O(1/K) for light-tailed stopping time distributions.

